---
import { codeToHtml } from "shiki";

import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";

const root = "/temporal-research-agent";
const video = `${root}/demo/out/temporal-literature-review-demo.mp4`;
const poster = `${root}/deck-assets/worker-killed.png`;
const codeThemes = {
  light: "github-light-high-contrast",
  dark: "github-dark-high-contrast",
} as const;
const beforeCode = await codeToHtml(
  `phase('Research')
await pipeline(
  EPS,
  // Stage 1: research
  (it) => agent(\`Research ONE podcast episode...\`),
  // Stage 2: deep dive
  (research, it) => agent(\`Write a rigorous DEEP-DIVE...\`),
  // Stage 3: script
  (_dd, it) => agent(\`Write a podcast SCRIPT...\`),
)

phase('Synthesis')
await parallel(literatureReviews)`,
  { lang: "javascript", themes: codeThemes },
);
const afterCode = await codeToHtml(
  `researched = await workflow.execute_activity(
    "research_episode", request, ...)
deep_dive = await workflow.execute_activity(
    "write_deep_dive", DeepDiveRequest(...), ...)
script = await workflow.execute_activity(
    "write_podcast_script", ScriptRequest(...), ...)

series_reviews = await asyncio.gather(
    *(self._write_series_review(...)
      for series in pipeline.series)
)`,
  { lang: "python", themes: codeThemes },
);
---

<PageLayout
  title="Temporal brings a research pipeline back to life"
  kicker="Temporal case study"
  intro="Improving the durability of our research pipeline using Temporal's Python SDK"
  description="A faithful rewrite of a podcast research pipeline as a Python Temporal Workflow, with complete source, recorded failure evidence, and implementation notes."
  image={poster}
  noindex={true}
>
  <article class="tra">
    <nav class="tra__tabs" aria-label="Submission materials">
      <a href={`${root}/code`}><span>01</span> Code</a>
      <a href={`${root}/readme`}><span>02</span> README</a>
      <a href={`${root}/deck.html`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>03</span> Deck</a>
      <a href={`${root}/research-output`}><span>04</span> Output</a>
    </nav>

    <section class="tra__section tra__summary">
      <header class="tra__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">Executive summary</p>
        <h2>An existing research product, rebuilt around durable execution</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="tra__copy prose">
      <p>
        For this assignment, I selected an existing research pipeline that used
        my <a href="https://www.sjarmak.ai/projects/code-intelligence-digest">Code Intelligence Digest</a>
        and <a href="https://www.sjarmak.ai/projects/scix-agent">SciX</a> MCP
        projects. The JavaScript program researched podcast episodes across two
        series. Each episode moved through research, a technical deep dive, and
        a podcast script. After those branches finished, it wrote one literature
        review per series.
      </p>
      <p>
        I reimplemented that business flow as a Python application built on
        Temporal. A Temporal Workflow owns episode order, bounded concurrency,
        completion policy, progress, series synthesis, and the final result.
        Temporal Activities own MCP calls, writer calls, heartbeats, clock
        reads, and artifact I/O. A Worker registers both on one task queue.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tested recovery by killing the Worker while two research Activities
        were running. A replacement Worker received Activity attempt 2, and the
        same Workflow Execution completed. A separate one-episode live run
        confirms that the input-driven Workflow still produces the full
        research brief, deep dive, podcast script, series synthesis, and
        provenance manifest.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="code-comparison" class="tra__section tra__numbered">
      <header class="tra__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">01</p>
        <h2>The code sample before Temporal</h2>
        <p>
          The original program contains the complete domain model: episode
          briefs, seed papers, research rules, deep-dive and script contracts,
          and final literature reviews.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="tra__code">
        <section>
          <h3>Before source: process-local JavaScript</h3>
          <div class="tra__code-render" set:html={beforeCode} />
          <p>
            The graph was valid, but one client process held the active phase,
            pipeline cursor, and in-flight promises.
          </p>
          <a href={`${root}/code/before`}>Open the complete annotated JavaScript</a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h3>After source: durable Python orchestration</h3>
          <div class="tra__code-render" set:html={afterCode} />
          <p>
            The Python rewrite preserves the dependency graph while moving its
            durable control flow into a Temporal Workflow.
          </p>
          <a href={`${root}/code`}>Open all annotated Python files</a>
        </section>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tra__section tra__numbered">
      <header class="tra__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">02</p>
        <h2>The Temporal implementation</h2>
        <p>
          Deterministic orchestration stays in the Workflow. External and
          nondeterministic work stays in Activities.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="tra__architecture">
        <article>
          <h3>Temporal Service</h3>
          <p>Persists Event History, Workflow state, task delivery, and durable timers.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Workflow</h3>
          <p>Owns ordering, bounded fan-out, completion policy, progress, and series-level fan-in.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Activities</h3>
          <p>Run MCP retrieval, writer calls, heartbeats, clock reads, and artifact I/O.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Worker</h3>
          <p>Polls the task queue and executes the registered Workflow and Activities.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="tra__copy prose">
        <p>
          Large documents remain outside Event History. Frozen typed dataclasses
          carry stable IDs, result status, content hashes, and artifact
          references between Workflow and Activity boundaries. Episode
          definitions remain input data, so the same Workflow can run one
          episode, a selected set, or the full historical input.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each episode executes <code>research_episode</code>,
          <code>write_deep_dive</code>, and
          <code>write_podcast_script</code> in order. Completed branches fan in
          to <code>write_series_review</code>, followed by the provenance
          manifest.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tra__section tra__numbered">
      <header class="tra__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">03</p>
        <h2>Recovery proof</h2>
        <p>
          The recovery script waits until two research Activities are
          heartbeating, kills the Worker with SIGKILL, starts a replacement,
          and waits on the original Workflow ID and Run ID.
        </p>
      </header>
      <figure class="tra__media">
        <video controls preload="metadata" poster={poster}>
          <source src={video} type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
        <figcaption>
          The Worker dies with two research Activities in flight. Temporal Web
          shows both retries and the completed episode and series-review outputs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="tra__evidence">
        <figure>
          <button
            class="tra__expand"
            type="button"
            data-expand-image
            aria-label="Expand the Worker failure screenshot"
          >
            <img
              src={`${root}/deck-assets/worker-killed.png`}
              alt="Terminal showing the Worker killed while two Activities are in flight"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <span>Open full view</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The Workflow remains open after the Worker receives SIGKILL.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <button
            class="tra__expand"
            type="button"
            data-expand-image
            aria-label="Expand the completed Workflow screenshot"
          >
            <img
              src={`${root}/deck-assets/workflow-completed.png`}
              alt="Temporal Web showing the original Workflow Execution completed"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <span>Open full view</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The replacement Worker completes the original Workflow Execution.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <button
            class="tra__expand"
            type="button"
            data-expand-image
            aria-label="Expand the Temporal Web retry screenshot"
          >
            <img
              src={`${root}/deck-assets/activity-attempt-two.png`}
              alt="Temporal Web showing two research Activities starting on attempt two"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <span>Open full view</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>Event History shows both <code>research_episode</code> Activities on attempt 2.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <dl class="tra__run">
        <div><dt>Workflow ID</dt><dd>temporal-phasee-demo-1785443614</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Completed</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Episodes</dt><dd>mas-ep4, code-ep4</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Failures</dt><dd>None</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="tra__section tra__numbered">
      <header class="tra__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">04</p>
        <h2>Design choices and tradeoffs</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="tra__tradeoffs">
        <article>
          <h3>Retries and timeouts</h3>
          <p>
            Activities allow three attempts with bounded exponential backoff.
            Research and writing use Start-to-Close and Heartbeat Timeouts. The
            short demo values make recovery visible; production values should
            reflect provider latency, cost, and rate limits.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>At-least-once execution</h3>
          <p>
            MCP requests use stable hashes and a write-once response journal.
            Read-only duplicate retrieval wastes work without changing external
            state. Paid or mutating calls would also need provider-enforced
            idempotency.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Artifacts and History</h3>
          <p>
            Stable references keep large research documents out of Event
            History. The sample filesystem store is inspectable; multiple
            Worker hosts would require shared object storage or a database.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Runtime boundary</h3>
          <p>
            The live integration runs on the configured workstation. SciX and
            Code Intelligence Digest depend on local indexes and services that
            are not part of this public documentation package.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tra__section tra__numbered">
      <header class="tra__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">05</p>
        <h2>Results and verification</h2>
        <p>
          The recovery run proves durable execution. The separate live run
          proves that the same generic Workflow still produces a complete
          research product.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="tra__results">
        <article>
          <p class="kicker">Recovery run</p>
          <strong>2 episode branches</strong>
          <p>Two series reviews and one manifest completed after both research Activities reached attempt 2.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="kicker">Live product run</p>
          <strong>29 records</strong>
          <p>One research brief, deep dive, podcast script, series synthesis, and provenance manifest completed with no failed episodes.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="kicker">Quality gates</p>
          <strong>Strict and typed</strong>
          <p>Frozen dataclasses, strict MyPy, Ruff, and branch-aware tests above 80 percent coverage.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="tra__output-link">
        <a href={`${root}/research-output`}>Inspect the original and Temporal research products <span>→</span></a>
      </p>
    </section>

  </article>

  <dialog id="evidence-viewer" class="tra-viewer" aria-label="Expanded evidence screenshot">
    <button class="tra-viewer__close" type="button" data-close-viewer>
      Close <span aria-hidden="true">×</span>
    </button>
    <figure>
      <img alt="" />
      <figcaption></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </dialog>
</PageLayout>

<script>
  const viewer = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>("#evidence-viewer");
  const viewerImage = viewer?.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>("img");
  const viewerCaption = viewer?.querySelector<HTMLElement>("figcaption");
  const closeButton = viewer?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-close-viewer]");

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-expand-image]").forEach((trigger) => {
    trigger.addEventListener("click", () => {
      const image = trigger.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>("img");
      const caption = trigger.closest("figure")?.querySelector("figcaption");
      if (!viewer || !viewerImage || !viewerCaption || !image) return;

      viewerImage.src = image.currentSrc || image.src;
      viewerImage.alt = image.alt;
      viewerCaption.textContent = caption?.textContent ?? "";
      viewer.showModal();
    });
  });

  closeButton?.addEventListener("click", () => viewer?.close());
  viewer?.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    if (event.target === viewer) viewer.close();
  });
</script>

<style>
